## Optimistic Concurrency Control

Implement the OCC model defined in DESIGN.md.

The client must send the version it believes it is updating.

Conceptually:

stored currentVersion = 3
request expectedVersion = 3

        ↓

UPDATE succeeds
currentVersion = 4
AreaVersion(version=4, UPDATED)

But:

stored currentVersion = 4
request expectedVersion = 3

        ↓

409 VERSION_CONFLICT

Do not silently overwrite newer data.

Do not implement last-write-wins.

Review DESIGN.md carefully to determine whether concurrency protection is
implemented through:

- an explicit conditional UPDATE;
- JPA optimistic locking;
- another documented mechanism.

Follow the design rather than inventing a different model.

Explain the exact SQL/JPA concurrency behavior in the plan.

---

## Concurrent Requests

The implementation must remain correct when two requests attempt to update
the same Area from the same starting version.

Example:

Area.currentVersion = 5

Request A expectedVersion=5
Request B expectedVersion=5

Exactly one request may successfully create version 6.

The other request must receive:

409 VERSION_CONFLICT

There must never be two AreaVersion rows with:

(area_id, version) = (same Area, 6)

Explain how the existing database constraints participate in enforcing this
invariant.

